  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1203 PM MDT Fri Oct 9 2026\n\nModels are in good agreement with satellite imagery showing the low \ndescending across British Columbia. They concur with the low \npushing into Washington State Saturday morning, but are already \nshowing increasing dispersion in solutions on timing and location \namong the models and even among the members of the ensembles early \nin the run. This dispersion is reducing confidence in the details of \nthis weekend system to include the track of the remnants of Rachel \nas it moves into Utah and Nevada. Stay tuned as the forecast \ndevelops, but confidence is high for gusty southwest winds \nincreasing Saturday afternoon as the trough develops over \nCalifornia. H700 winds will be about 30 kts Saturday increasing to \nclose to 50 kts by Sunday afternoon while the H500 winds increase \nfrom 35 kts to 60 kts over the same period. Expect winds 35 to\n50 mph Sunday afternoon with gust 50 to 60 mph possible\nespecially over the higher terrain. While pwats aren't real \nimpressive through this period at 0.70 to an inch, when put with\nthe winds aloft, the atmospheric river effects are significant \nwith Integrated Water Vapor Transport (IVT) values of 500 to 700\nkg/m/s. Expect heavy rain possible on the southwest face of \nrising terrain across eastern Utah and Eastern Colorado with \nthis strong orographic forcing. Additionally, the left exit \nregion of an 80 kt jet streak moves up over the region Sunday \nafternoon and night providing upper level dynamics supporting \npossible severe thunderstorms. The primary threat will be \ndamaging outflow winds in excess of 60 mph. Can't rule out large\nhail possible, or even a tornado with the upper level \nconvective support and strong shear.\n\nThe next system to impact the region is an early season winter storm \nas the cold-core Pacific Northwest low drops down through the Great \nbasin before lifting to the northeast across the region Tuesday \nevening and Wednesday. This won't bring a lot of snow to the region, \nbut we will likely see some winter driving conditions on the \nmountain passes with low visibilities and periods of slick roads. We \nwill likely see snow down to 8,000 ft, but don't expect any \naccumulation below about 10,000 ft. Stay tuned for updates on this \none too.",
